**Read:** [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), chapter 2 in full over this section; [Vacuum systems — Vincent Baglin and Roberto Kersevan](../sources/Vacuum-Systems-Baglin-Kersevan.lingua#r_1K9ATEHFM7ET2H8TRHC3QNCRFN), II.8.3.3; [Vacuum technology for superconducting devices — Paolo Chiggiato](../sources/Vacuum-for-Superconducting-Devices-Chiggiato.lingua#r_2PJTDCZXJ91HF8Q1BBY9FNMWH9), section 3.

A pump changes the population of gas molecules at its inlet. A transfer pump moves them toward an exhaust; a capture pump stores them on or within another material. Neither makes matter disappear. Follow the removed constituent through the whole apparatus, including backing equipment, traps and regeneration.

Begin with the inlet state and required service, then ask how a mechanism works in that regime. A displacement machine, a moving molecular surface and a cryogenic sorbent do not share one universal performance curve. Startup, steady operation, interruption and restoration may require different combinations.

**Work:** Draw the complete path of a water molecule and a hydrogen molecule through two alternative pumping arrangements. Mark where each is transferred, condensed, adsorbed or chemically retained. Identify where its inventory can later re-enter the chamber and what continuing utilities keep the intended state stable.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
**Continue in gas and vacuum systems:** [Displacement pumps and the meaning of swept volume](Displacement-pumps-and-the-meaning-of-swept-volume.lingua#r_3QZE6P8P0MT4J12RQKK39GJCQE).

